{"cuisine/somali-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/somali-cuisine","n":"Somali cuisine","c":"#e46a3f","crumb":[["Africa","cuisines#africa"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Somalia","intro":[["Somali cuisine is characterized by a history of maritime trade networks, nomadic culture, and a colonial influence. The use of spices reflect these elements and play a role in Somali cuisine. Pork consumption is forbidden in Somalia in accordance with sharia, as the vast majority of the population are Muslims. Meats such as beef, goat, lamb, camel, and fish, are always prepared halal."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"somali-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Somali_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Xawaash","ref":"site/xawaash-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"xawaash.com","note":"Somali recipe blog started in 2011 by Leila Adde and Abdullahi Kassim in Ontario.","url":"http://xawaash.com/","suggested":true}]}],"cont":"African cuisine"},"cuisine/udupi-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/udupi-cuisine","n":"Udupi cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Udupi cuisine (also known as Udipi) is a cuisine of South India. It forms an important part of Tuluva cuisine and takes its name from Udupi, a city on the southwest coast of India in the Tulunad region of the state of Karnataka."],["Most of Udupi cuisine is strictly vegetarian.  However, other elements of Udupi cuisine are non-vegetarian and common to Mangalorean cuisine."],["Udupi cuisine has its origin in the Udupi Sri Krishna Matha founded by Madhvacharya. Shivalli Brahmins are famous for \"Udupi hotels\" (vegetarian restaurants) known for serving typical south Indian dishes like idli, vada, dosa, shira and upma. The cuisine was developed by Madhva Brahmins to offer different kinds of food to Krishna at the Udupi Krishna Temple.  Hence, Udupi cuisine is also synonymously known as Shivalli Madhva cuisine."],["Udupi cuisine comprises dishes made primarily from grains, beans, vegetables, and fruits. The variety and range of dishes is wide, and a hallmark of the cuisine involves the use of locally available ingredients. The famous dosa (thick crisp pancakes, plain or masala when stuffed with potatoes) is said to have originated from this cuisine."],["Following the tradition of chaaturmasa vrata, which is a restriction of certain food ingredients in a certain period or season, may have led to the innovation of a variety of dishes in Udupi cuisine. Pumpkins and gourds are the main ingredients in sambar, a stew prepared with ground coconut and coconut oil as its base."],["The ubiquitous Indian dish dosa has its origins in Udupi, according to P. Thankappan Nair. Saaru, a spicy pepper water, is another essential part of the menu, and so are jackfruit, colocasia leaves, raw green bananas, mango pickle, red chillies, and salt. Adyes (dumplings), ajadinas (dry curries or stir fry curries), and chutneys, including one made of the skin of the ridge gourd, are specialities."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"udupi-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Udupi_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Udupi Recipes","ref":"site/udupi-recipes-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"udupi-recipes.com","note":"Blog dedicated to traditional Udupi and coastal Karnataka vegetarian dishes.","url":"https://udupi-recipes.com/about-me","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Masala dosa","dish/masala-dosa"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Rice","ingredient/rice"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Karnataka cuisine","cuisine/karnataka-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b4/Neer_Dose_-_Hotel_Latha%2C_Kota%2C_Udupi_-_Karnataka_-_PXL2415.jpg/500px-Neer_Dose_-_Hotel_Latha%2C_Kota%2C_Udupi_-_Karnataka_-_PXL2415.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"Harikrishnan N","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neer_Dose_-_Hotel_Latha%2C_Kota%2C_Udupi_-_Karnataka_-_PXL2415.jpg"}},"cuisine/emirati-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/emirati-cuisine","n":"Emirati cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of United Arab Emirates","intro":[["Emirati cuisine is the local traditional Arabic cuisine of the United Arab Emirates. The origins of Emirati cuisine come from the Bedouins who roamed the country. It is part of Eastern Arabian cuisine and shares similarities with cuisines from neighbouring countries, such as Omani cuisine and Saudi Arabian cuisine, as well as influences from different Middle Eastern and Asian cuisines.  As a major international hub, the United Arab Emirates today has a multicultural variety of cuisines from different parts of the world."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"emirati-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirati_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Emirati Recipes (Dubai Culture)","ref":"site/dubaiculture-gov-ae","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dubaiculture.gov.ae","note":"Dubai Culture and Arts Authority project documenting traditional Emirati recipes.","url":"https://dubaiculture.gov.ae/en/about-us/special-projects/our-projects-events/Emirati-Recipes","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Eastern Arabian cuisine","cuisine/eastern-arabian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/ae/Luqaimat.jpg/500px-Luqaimat.jpg","w":500,"h":500,"by":"Wikiemirati","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luqaimat.jpg"}},"dish/adega":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/adega","n":"Adega","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Portuguese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Adega is a Portuguese restaurant in the Little Portugal neighborhood of San Jose, California, United States. Established in 2015, it became the first restaurant in San Jose to be awarded a Michelin star, earning one star in 2016. In mid-2023, the owners announced that the restaurant would close at the end of the year, to be replaced by a second location of Petiscos, a more casual Portuguese restaurant with the same chefs. Ownership was transferred in October 2024 and announced Adega would reopen in November 2024."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-adega"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Adega"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Food From Portugal","ref":"site/foodfromportugal-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run recipe site and video channel of traditional Portuguese dishes.","url":"https://www.foodfromportugal.com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Portuguese cuisine","links":[["Alheira","dish/alheira"],["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"],["Arroz de marisco","dish/arroz-de-marisco"],["Bacalhau à Brás","dish/bacalhau-a-bras"],["Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá","dish/bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa"],["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"],["Bacalhau à Narcisa","dish/bacalhau-a-narcisa"],["Bacalhau à Zé do Pipo","dish/bacalhau-a-ze-do-pipo"],["Bacalhau com natas","dish/bacalhau-com-natas"],["Batatas a murro","dish/batatas-a-murro"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bolo de bolacha","dish/bolo-de-bolacha"],["Canja de galinha","dish/canja-de-galinha"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Encharcada","dish/encharcada"],["Fartura","dish/fartura"],["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"],["Fios de ovos","dish/fios-de-ovos"],["Francesinha","dish/francesinha"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Pão Alentejano","dish/pao-alentejano"],["Pão de Ló","dish/pao-de-lo"],["Pataniscas","dish/pataniscas"],["Polvo à lagareiro","dish/polvo-a-lagareiro"],["Prego (Portuguese sandwich)","dish/prego-portuguese-sandwich"],["Pudim Abade de Priscos","dish/pudim-abade-de-priscos"],["Pudim Molotov","dish/pudim-molotov"],["Sericaia","dish/sericaia"],["Serradura","dish/serradura"],["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/6/65/Adega_San_Jose.jpg/500px-Adega_San_Jose.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Yngvadottir","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adega_San_Jose.jpg"}},"ingredient/cut-of-pork":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cut-of-pork","n":"Cut of pork","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["The cuts of pork are the different parts of the pig which are consumed as food by humans. The terminology and extent of each cut varies from country to country. There are between four and six primal cuts, which are the large parts in which the pig is first cut: the shoulder (blade and picnic), loin, belly (spare ribs and side) and leg. These are often sold wholesale, as are other parts of the pig with less meat, such as the head, feet and tail. Retail cuts are the specific cuts which are used to obtain different kinds of meat, such as tenderloin and ham. There are at least 25 Iberian pork cuts, including jamón."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cut-of-pork"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cut_of_pork"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Cassoeula","dish/cassoeula"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Pig's trotter","ingredient/pig-s-trotter"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Pork ribs","ingredient/pork-ribs"],["Pork rind","ingredient/pork-rind"],["Savoy cabbage","ingredient/savoy-cabbage"],["Tomato paste","ingredient/tomato-paste"]]}]},"dish/cock-a-leekie":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cock-a-leekie","n":"Cock-a-leekie","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Scottish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Cock-a-leekie soup is a Scottish soup dish consisting of leeks and peppered chicken stock, often thickened with rice, or sometimes barley. The original recipe added prunes during cooking, and traditionalists still garnish with a julienne of prunes."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cock-a-leekie"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cock-a-leekie"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Scottish cuisine","cuisine/scottish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Chicken stock","ingredient/chicken-stock"],["Leek","ingredient/leek"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Scottish Scran","ref":"site/scottishscran-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"scottishscran.com","note":"Blog working through traditional Scottish dishes one recipe at a time.","url":"https://scottishscran.com","suggested":true},{"name":"Something Sweet Something Savoury: Scottish recipes","ref":"site/somethingsweetsomethingsavoury-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"somethingsweetsomethingsavoury.com","note":"Scottish home baking and cooking section of a UK food blog.","url":"https://somethingsweetsomethingsavoury.com/category/scottish-recipes/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Scottish cuisine","links":[["Clootie dumpling","dish/clootie-dumpling"],["Finnan haddie","dish/finnan-haddie"],["Haggis","dish/haggis"],["Haggis pakora","dish/haggis-pakora"],["Hobnob biscuit","dish/hobnob-biscuit"],["Lorne sausage","dish/lorne-sausage"],["Mince and tatties","dish/mince-and-tatties"],["Rumbledethumps","dish/rumbledethumps"],["Scotch broth","dish/scotch-broth"],["Skirlie","dish/skirlie"],["Stornoway black pudding","dish/stornoway-black-pudding"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/80/Cock-a-leekie_Soup.jpg/500px-Cock-a-leekie_Soup.jpg","w":500,"h":374,"by":"Laurel F","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cock-a-leekie_Soup.jpg"}},"ingredient/crepe":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/crepe","n":"Crêpe","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 2 dishes across 3 cuisines","intro":[["A crêpe is a dish made from unleavened batter or dough that is cooked on a frying pan or a griddle. Crêpes are usually one of two varieties: sweet crêpes (crêpes sucrées) or savoury crêpes (galettes; crêpes salées). They are often served with a wide variety of fillings such as cheese, fruit, vegetables, meats, and a variety of spreads. Crêpes can also be flambéed, such as in Crêpes Suzette."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-crepe"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cr%C3%AApe"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":3,"groups":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine",[["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"]]],["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Lumpia semarang","dish/lumpia-semarang"]]],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine",[["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Crêpes","ingredient/crepes"],["Curaçao liqueur","ingredient/curacao-liqueur"],["Grand Marnier","ingredient/grand-marnier"],["Orange (fruit)","ingredient/orange-fruit"],["Tangerine","ingredient/tangerine"],["Triple sec","ingredient/triple-sec"],["Zest","ingredient/zest"],["Bamboo shoot","ingredient/bamboo-shoot"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Dried shrimp","ingredient/dried-shrimp"],["Lumpia wrapper","ingredient/lumpia-wrapper"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/0/09/Crepes_dsc07085.jpg/500px-Crepes_dsc07085.jpg","w":500,"h":302,"by":"David Monniaux","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crepes_dsc07085.jpg"}},"dish/curry-bread":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/curry-bread","n":"Curry bread","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Japanese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Curry bread is a popular Japanese food consisting of Japanese curry (or other types of curry) wrapped in a piece of dough, which is then coated in bread crumbs and deep fried. On occasion it is baked instead of deep-fried, but deep-frying is the most common method of cooking. Curry bread is usually found in bakeries and convenience stores."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-curry-bread"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curry_bread"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Japanese cuisine","cuisine/japanese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Bread crumbs","ingredient/bread-crumbs"],["Japanese curry","ingredient/japanese-curry"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Just One Cookbook","ref":"site/justonecookbook-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"justonecookbook.com","note":"Namiko Chen's long-running blog and videos on Japanese home cooking.","url":"https://justonecookbook.com","suggested":true},{"name":"RecipeTin Japan","ref":"site/japan-recipetineats-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"japan.recipetineats.com","note":"Japanese home recipes from Yumiko, a Japan-born cook, sister site to RecipeTin Eats.","url":"https://japan.recipetineats.com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Japanese cuisine","links":[["Benihana","dish/benihana"],["Bergh–Stoutenburgh House","dish/bergh-stoutenburgh-house"],["Bincho","dish/bincho"],["Boxer Ramen","dish/boxer-ramen"],["Cagen","dish/cagen"],["Chikuwa","dish/chikuwa"],["Donburi","dish/donburi"],["Em (restaurant)","dish/em-restaurant"],["Esaki (restaurant)","dish/esaki-restaurant"],["Feng Sushi","dish/feng-sushi"],["Freshness Burger","dish/freshness-burger"],["Fukamushicha","dish/fukamushicha"],["Gyokuro","dish/gyokuro"],["Heihachi Jaya","dish/heihachi-jaya"],["Hitsumabushi","dish/hitsumabushi"],["HokBen","dish/hokben"],["Hokka Hokka Tei","dish/hokka-hokka-tei"],["Horumonyaki","dish/horumonyaki"],["Ichibanya","dish/ichibanya"],["Ichigo Ichie","dish/ichigo-ichie"],["Ichimura at Brushstroke","dish/ichimura-at-brushstroke"],["Iekei ramen","dish/iekei-ramen"],["Inago no tsukudani","dish/inago-no-tsukudani"],["Ishikawa (restaurant)","dish/ishikawa-restaurant"],["Italian Tomato","dish/italian-tomato"],["Itamae","dish/itamae"],["Itsu","dish/itsu"],["Ivan Ramen","dish/ivan-ramen"],["Izakaya","dish/izakaya"],["Izumi (restaurant)","dish/izumi-restaurant"],["Japanese cheesecake","dish/japanese-cheesecake"],["Japanese noodles","dish/japanese-noodles"],["Japanese restaurant","dish/japanese-restaurant"],["Japanese tea","dish/japanese-tea"],["Jingisukan","dish/jingisukan"],["Jinya Ramen Bar","dish/jinya-ramen-bar"],["Kadowaki (restaurant)","dish/kadowaki-restaurant"],["Kaki furai","dish/kaki-furai"],["Kamaboko","dish/kamaboko"],["Kanda (restaurant)","dish/kanda-restaurant"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/37/Curry-bun%2Ccurry-pan%2Ckatori-city%2Cjapan.JPG/500px-Curry-bun%2Ccurry-pan%2Ckatori-city%2Cjapan.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"katorisi","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Curry-bun%2Ccurry-pan%2Ckatori-city%2Cjapan.JPG"}},"dish/halawet-el-jibn":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/halawet-el-jibn","n":"Halawet el jibn","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Syrian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Halawet el-jibn is a Syrian dessert made of a semolina and cheese dough, filled with qishta. Its 